#:blog{:title
       "DaoStream: Inspired by Plan 9, Simpler Than 9P",
       :date #inst "2026-08-29T00:00:00.000-00:00",
       :abstract
       [:p
        "Plan 9's 9P required 13 message types and server-side state to maintain the illusion that everything is a file. DaoStream keeps Plan 9's discipline—one small verb set over every medium—but replaces the file with the log, the fid with a reader-owned cursor, and blocking calls with outcomes as data."],
       :content
       [:section.blog-article
        [:div.section-inner
         [:article
          :$blog-title
          :$blog-date
          [:h2 "The Plan 9 Dream"]
          [:p
           "In the late 1980s, the creators of Unix at Bell Labs asked themselves: what if we took \"everything is a file\" seriously? "
           [:em "Really"]
           " seriously?"]
          [:p "The result was Plan 9, an operating system where:"]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li "Network connections are files you " [:code "read()"] " and " [:code "write()"]]
           [:li "Process control is writing commands to " [:code "/proc/123/ctl"]]
           [:li "Graphics are drawing commands written to " [:code "/dev/draw/data"]]
           [:li "Even window management is file operations"]]
          [:p
           "One API—" [:code "open()"] ", " [:code "read()"] ", " [:code "write()"] ", " [:code "close()"] "—for everything."]
          [:p "It was beautiful. It was elegant. It almost worked."]

          [:h2 "The Problem: 9P Had to Pretend Everything Was a File"]
          [:p
           "To make \"everything is a file\" work over networks, Plan 9 invented the "
           [:strong "9P protocol"]
           ". And 9P is... complicated. It has "
           [:strong "13 message types"]
           ": version negotiation, attach, multi-hop path walking, open, read, write, stat, wstat, create, remove, clunk, flush."]
          [:p
           "Why so complex? Because "
           [:strong "9P had to maintain the illusion that remote resources were files"]
           ":"]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li "Files have hierarchical paths → need " [:code "Twalk"] " to navigate " [:code "/net/tcp/clone"]]
           [:li "Files have permissions and owners → need " [:code "Tstat"] "/" [:code "Twstat"] " for metadata"]
           [:li "Files have handles (fids) → need server-side state management"]
           [:li "Files have read positions → the " [:em "server"] " remembers every client's offset"]]
          [:p
           "9P tried to make the network transparent by making everything look like POSIX files. "
           "This was both its genius and its burden."]

          [:h2 "What if We Don't Pretend?"]
          [:p
           "At Datom.world, we're building on Plan 9's philosophy, but we asked a different question: "
           [:strong "what is the honest universal abstraction for communication and IO?"]]
          [:p
           "Files are wonderful for persistent, randomly-accessible storage. But a file is a mutable byte array: "
           "read offset 100 twice and you may get two different answers, and the old bytes are simply gone. "
           "Communication is not like that. Communication is temporal and append-only—things "
           [:em "happen"]
           ", in an order, and never un-happen."]
          [:p
           "So DaoStream keeps Plan 9's discipline—one small verb set over every heterogeneous medium—but changes the noun: "
           [:strong "everything is a log"]
           ". An append-only, positioned sequence of values that readers observe without consuming."]

          [:h2 "The Contract, Verb for Verb"]
          [:p
           "The DaoStream contract maps almost verb-for-verb onto Plan 9—every difference is a deliberate correction:"]
          [:table
           [:thead
            [:tr [:th "Plan 9"] [:th "DaoStream"] [:th "What changed"]]]
           [:tbody
            [:tr
             [:td "path / name"]
             [:td "portable descriptor"]
             [:td "Self-contained value (CORBA-IOR-like), not a position in a global tree"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:code "create"] " / " [:code "open"]]
             [:td [:code "create!"] " / " [:code "attach!"]]
             [:td "Creation is not attachment; attaching never creates"]]
            [:tr
             [:td "fid"]
             [:td "realization"]
             [:td "Host-local live value; never travels, never pretends to"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:code "read"]]
             [:td [:code "next"]]
             [:td "Total and non-blocking; observes, never consumes"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:code "write"]]
             [:td [:code "append!"]]
             [:td "Append-only; nothing is ever overwritten in place"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:code "close"]]
             [:td [:code "close!"]]
             [:td "Ends future appends; erases no retained history"]]
            [:tr
             [:td "fd offset (server-side)"]
             [:td "cursor (reader-owned)"]
             [:td "The biggest fix—see below"]]]]

          [:h2 "The Cursor: What the fid's Offset Should Have Been"]
          [:p
           "Here is 9P's deepest statefulness: when a client reads a file, the "
           [:em "server"]
           " remembers the offset. The client says \"read next,\" and the server maintains cursor state for every fid of every client. "
           "Server crash? All positions lost. Client migrates? Re-attach, re-open, re-seek. "
           "And in Unix, a forked child "
           [:em "shares"]
           " its parent's offset—two readers aliasing one position, interleaving destructively."]
          [:p "DaoStream inverts the ownership entirely:"]
          [:div.code-block
           [:pre
            [:code
             ";; the stream mints an initial cursor; the READER owns it\n"
             "(cursor R)\n"
             ";; => {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n"
             ";;     :dao.stream/cursor  c}\n\n"
             ";; observing hands you a successor cursor\n"
             "(next R c)\n"
             ";; => {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n"
             ";;     :dao.stream/value   v\n"
             ";;     :dao.stream/cursor  c'}"]]]
          [:p
           "A cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it. Advancing is "
           [:em "receiving a successor"]
           ", never mutating. The stream holds no reader positions at all—dropping a cursor "
           [:em "is"]
           " detaching, with nothing to clunk, time out, or reference-count. "
           "Share history with another interpreter by handing over a cursor: it's a value, so sharing copies, never aliases."]
          [:p
           "Every piece of 9P lifecycle machinery—" [:code "Tclunk"] ", timeouts, dead-client cleanup—existed to manage state "
           "that DaoStream simply refuses to keep."]

          [:h2 "Reading Never Destroys"]
          [:p
           "Because the stream is a log and cursors are private, reading is non-destructive by construction. "
           "Any number of readers observe the same retained values independently; none can starve another. "
           "There is no destructive read anywhere in the contract—no drain, no take. "
           "An operation that removed an element as it read would make one reader's progress every other reader's data loss."]
          [:p
           "When retention is bounded and a slow reader falls behind the window, the stream doesn't lie and doesn't silently reposition anyone. It reports, as data:"]
          [:div.code-block
           [:pre
            [:code
             "{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/gap\n"
             " :dao.stream/cursor  oldest-retained}"]]]
          [:p
           "The reader decides what missing history means—fatal for a replayer, a shrug for a live dashboard. "
           "A mutable file destroys history silently every time it's written. A DaoStream gap is the log being honest about the one way history can leave."]

          [:h2 "Outcomes Are Data, Not Blocking and errno"]
          [:p
           "A Plan 9 " [:code "read"] " on an empty pipe " [:em "blocks the process"] "—waiting is the substrate's control-flow mechanism, and the network hides inside it. "
           "DaoStream bans that. " [:code "next"] " is total: every state of the stream has an immediate data answer."]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li [:code ":dao.stream/ok"] " — a value, and your successor cursor"]
           [:li [:code ":dao.stream/blocked"] " — nothing here yet; retry when " [:em "you"] " choose"]
           [:li [:code ":dao.stream/end"] " — closed, and you've read past the retained tail"]
           [:li [:code ":dao.stream/gap"] " — evicted history, with a recovery cursor"]]
          [:p
           "Writes are the same: " [:code "append!"] " answers " [:code ":dao.stream/full"] " or " [:code ":dao.stream/closed"] " as values, never as exceptions. "
           "Who waits, and how long, is an interpreter or runtime decision made " [:em "above"] " the stream. The substrate never captures your thread and never calls you back."]

          [:h2 "Capability Honesty vs. the ctl File"]
          [:p
           "Plan 9 forced every resource into full file shape even when the resource couldn't honor it. The escape hatch was the "
           [:code "ctl"]
           " file: to dial the network you wrote the string "
           [:code "\"connect 204.62.29.1!80\""]
           " into "
           [:code "/net/tcp/n/ctl"]
           "—a little command language smuggled through a byte interface, untyped and unvalidated."]
          [:p "DaoStream splits that idiom into typed, validated parts:"]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li
            "The " [:strong "creation specification"] " is the ctl-write made explicit: data that says how to build a stream, validated by the transport, one shot."]
           [:li
            "The " [:strong "portable descriptor"] " only " [:em "names"] " an existing stream—never executable code, never a construction program."]
           [:li
            "A " [:strong "realization declares its capabilities"] ": reader, writer, closable—the subset its medium can honor."]]
          [:p
           "That last point is where the file metaphor quietly broke and DaoStream refuses to paper over it. A raw socket retains nothing—it cannot honor cursors, re-reading, or independent readers. So a WebSocket realization is "
           [:em "writer and closable only"]
           ". Outbound, sending is appending. Inbound, each arriving message is deposited onto a log "
           [:em "the caller composed"]
           "—a ring buffer you sized, beside the socket, never hidden inside it. One conversation is a pipe with a log at each end, each end owned by the party that reads it. "
           "Where 9P faked file semantics over sockets, DaoStream declares what each transport honestly is."]

          [:h2 "Composition: The Pipe Is Dumb"]
          [:p
           "Plan 9's other great composition story was the pipeline, and DaoStream keeps its exact division of labor: the pipe is dumb; the transforming happens in the processes at either end."]
          [:p
           "DaoStream defines no transformer, no combinator, no derived-stream machinery. Anything that transforms one stream into another is an "
           [:strong "interpreter"]
           "—it reads its input through its own cursor and appends to an ordinary output stream. "
           "Reliability, ordering, deduplication, persistence: none are protocol features, all are interpreters you compose when your use case needs them."]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li "Sensor readings might flow raw—latest value wins, drops don't matter"]
           [:li "A database log gets an ordering interpreter that buffers and sorts before appending downstream"]
           [:li "A chat gets retry and dedup interpreters, but no persistence"]]
          [:p
           "In TCP and 9P these guarantees are mandatory protocol features everyone pays for. Here every guarantee is a process you can see, chose, and could have left out."]

          [:h2 "State at the Edges"]
          [:p
           "This is the deepest difference, and it's philosophical. 9P "
           [:strong "bakes state transition semantics into the protocol"]
           ": fids, offsets, open-file tables, authentication context. Every server must implement the same state machine; every client depends on it; none of it survives a crash or a migration."]
          [:p
           "DaoStream's substrate keeps exactly one kind of state: "
           [:strong "the log itself"]
           ". Each logical stream is one append-only sequence—a successfully appended value occupies exactly one definite position, and every operation's outcome reflects the sequence at one moment. The contract guarantees nothing further about concurrency, because there is nothing further to guarantee: the sequence is the one truth, each outcome is one perspective on it, and what an ordering "
           [:em "means"]
           "—including any coordination among writers—is interpretation, which happens above the stream."]
          [:p
           "There are no subscriptions in the substrate, no subscriber registries, no wake callbacks. A reader that sees "
           [:code ":dao.stream/blocked"]
           " polls again when it chooses. Semantics—replay, subscription, ordering, meaning itself—emerge from interpreters at the edges. Data is syntax; interpretation creates semantics."]
          [:p
           "One consequence reaches further than it first appears: DaoStream assigns stream elements "
           [:em "no intrinsic meaning at all"]
           ". Not bytes, not datoms—just values. The datom "
           [:code "[e a v t m]"]
           " is what "
           [:em "dao.space's"]
           " interpreters make of the values flowing beneath them. The substrate that carries facts is not the layer that knows they are facts."]

          [:h2 "The Refusal of Transparency"]
          [:p
           "9P's crown jewel was network transparency: mount a remote server and its files are indistinguishable from local ones. The network vanishes behind "
           [:code "read"]
           " and "
           [:code "write"]
           "—and so do its failure modes, hiding inside blocked system calls."]
          [:p
           "DaoStream deliberately declines the illusion. The boundary is a first-class fact with a precise location: "
           [:strong "serialization"]
           ". A realization—the live, host-local value—never crosses a serialization boundary. A descriptor—plain data, self-contained like a CORBA interoperable reference—exists precisely to cross them. Within one memory space, streams really are values that can be sent through streams: append a realization to an in-memory stream and the receiver simply uses it. The moment elements are encoded—for a wire "
           [:em "or a file"]
           "—only data survives, and a name must travel where the value cannot."]
          [:p
           "This is π-calculus mobility done honestly. An agent that discovers a descriptor in some stream it reads can "
           [:code "attach!"]
           " and join a conversation it didn't know existed at start time—the topology of communication evolves at runtime, carried by the data itself. "
           "But nothing pretends the remote is local: attaching returns outcomes as data, appends answer "
           [:code "full"]
           " when a send buffer is saturated, and a descriptor that names something unreachable resolves to an honest "
           [:code ":dao.stream/not-found"]
           "."]

          [:h2 "The Comparison"]
          [:table
           [:thead
            [:tr
             [:th "Aspect"]
             [:th "Plan 9"]
             [:th "DaoStream"]]]
           [:tbody
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "Philosophy"]]
             [:td "Everything is a file"]
             [:td "Everything is a log"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "Mutation"]]
             [:td "In place; history destroyed silently"]
             [:td "Append-only; loss is explicit (" [:code "gap"] ")"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "Reader position"]]
             [:td "Server-kept offset per fid"]
             [:td "Reader-owned immutable cursor"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "Waiting"]]
             [:td "Blocking " [:code "read"]]
             [:td "Total " [:code "next"] "; outcomes are data"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "State semantics"]]
             [:td "Protocol-defined (fids, offsets, lifecycle)"]
             [:td "Only the log; everything else is interpretation"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "Resource mismatch"]]
             [:td "Forced into file shape (" [:code "ctl"] " files)"]
             [:td "Declared capabilities (a socket is a writer)"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "Names"]]
             [:td "Paths in a mounted hierarchy"]
             [:td "Self-contained portable descriptors"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "Network"]]
             [:td "Transparent (and hidden) via 9P"]
             [:td "Explicit serialization boundary, crossed by data"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "Composition"]]
             [:td "Union mounts, pipelines"]
             [:td "Interpreters between ordinary streams"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "Mobility"]]
             [:td "Mount remote filesystems"]
             [:td "Descriptors through streams (π-calculus)"]]]]

          [:h2 "The Lesson"]
          [:p
           "Plan 9 taught us that "
           [:strong "universal interfaces are powerful"]
           ": one small verb set over everything is what makes composition possible at all."]
          [:p
           "But Plan 9 also taught us that "
           [:strong "forcing everything into one metaphor has costs"]
           ". 9P's 13 message types, its server-side state, its ctl-file command strings—all of it is the price of pretending that mutable, seekable, hierarchical files are the shape of every resource."]
          [:p
           "DaoStream keeps the discipline and changes the noun. The log fits what communication actually is—temporal, append-only, observed rather than consumed—so nothing has to be pretended:"]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li "History is kept, and its loss is reported, never silent"]
           [:li "Readers own their own progress; the substrate remembers no one"]
           [:li "Every operational answer is a value; nothing blocks, nothing calls back"]
           [:li "Every transport declares what it honestly is"]
           [:li "The boundary between here and there is data, not an illusion"]]
          [:p [:strong "Plan 9 showed us the power of universal interfaces."]]
          [:p
           [:strong "DaoStream shows that the interface can stay universal without the metaphor telling lies."]]]]]}
